4. CASE TEXT AND GROUNDING
--------------------------
AWPTCM T33249 "IPv4_ARP - ARP Logging".

STEP TEXT: NONE EXISTS.  No exact-title match in ck.db (checked at triage for all 13 campaign
titles; only T43849 has step text).

Grounded in:
  (a) X960 platform support TPS row 21 "ARP Logging" -- priority 1, "Yes", note column empty.
  (b) `ip_feature_overview_guide.pdf` (repo root `Feature Overview Guides/`), the ARP logging
      section (p9).
  (c) The DUT's own CLI, read-only `?` probe:
        awplus(config)# arp log ?
          mac-address-format  MAC address format
          <cr>
        awplus(config)# arp log mac-address-format ?
          ieee  Standard format for a MAC address
  (d) STANDING-ORDERS Section 7 and memory `satisfactory-level-of-testing`.

OBJECTIVE: with `arp log` configured, the DUT logs ARP cache events caused by real ARP traffic;
with it absent it logs none; the `mac-address-format ieee` sub-option changes how the MAC is
rendered; both are removable; and the setting survives a reboot and still logs afterwards.

METHOD NOTE: every leg below drives a COMPLETE, CRAFTED ARP resolution rather than inspecting a
table -- `tools/arpleg.py` sends routed probes that force the DUT to broadcast an ARP request,
and its `--answer` responder replies, so the DUT creates a genuine dynamic entry.  (That
responder is necessary: this DUT does not learn from a request it merely answers --
T33247/work/run1.log section 5.)  `clear log buffered` is run immediately before each leg, so
the log read afterwards holds that leg's events and nothing earlier.  `clear arp-cache` is run
with it, so each leg produces a `del` (the clear) and an `add` (the re-learn).


